-- Q1: medium / L2 — Section 501(h) expenditure test election for lobbying limits
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Riverdale Education Alliance, a 501(c)(3) with $400,000 in exempt purpose expenditures, makes the Section 501(h) election to use the expenditure test for lobbying. Under the expenditure test, what is the maximum amount Riverdale may spend on lobbying without jeopardizing its exempt status?', '["$80,000 (20% of exempt purpose expenditures)", "$40,000 (10% of exempt purpose expenditures)", "$20,000 (5% of exempt purpose expenditures)", "$200,000 (50% of exempt purpose expenditures)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(h), a 501(c)(3) organization electing the expenditure test may spend up to 20% of the first $500,000 of exempt purpose expenditures on lobbying. For Riverdale''s $400,000 in expenditures, the lobbying nontaxable amount is $80,000 (20% × $400,000). Unlike the vague "substantial part" test, the 501(h) election provides specific dollar thresholds with graduated percentages. The 10% figure confuses the lobbying limit with the grassroots lobbying sublimit, which is 25% of the overall lobbying nontaxable amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Form 990 vs. 990-EZ filing thresholds
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Brightview Arts Council, a 501(c)(3) public charity, has gross receipts of $180,000 and total assets of $450,000 for its 2025 tax year. Which annual information return must Brightview file with the IRS?', '["Form 990 (full return) because total assets exceed $200,000", "Form 990-EZ because gross receipts are under $200,000 and total assets are under $500,000", "Form 990-N (e-Postcard) because gross receipts are under $200,000", "No return is required because gross receipts are under $200,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 6033, Form 990-EZ is available for organizations with gross receipts under $200,000 AND total assets under $500,000. Brightview meets both thresholds ($180,000 receipts and $450,000 assets), so Form 990-EZ is the appropriate filing. Form 990 (full return) is required when gross receipts are $200,000 or more OR total assets are $500,000 or more. The e-Postcard (Form 990-N) is only for organizations with gross receipts of $50,000 or less, far below Brightview''s level.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Controlled entity UBIT (80% ownership rule)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'New Horizons Foundation, a 501(c)(3), owns 90% of a for-profit software company. The software company pays $200,000 in annual rent to the foundation for office space. The fair market value rental rate for comparable space is $150,000. How should the foundation treat this rental income for UBIT purposes?', '["All $200,000 is excluded as passive rental income from real property", "Only $150,000 (the fair market value portion) is included in UBIT", "Only the $50,000 excess over fair market value is included in UBIT", "The entire $200,000 is included in UBIT regardless of fair market value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(13), rent received from a controlled entity (80% or more ownership) is treated as UBIT to the extent the payment exceeds fair market value. Since New Horizons owns 90% (exceeding the 80% threshold) and the $200,000 rent exceeds the $150,000 FMV by $50,000, only the $50,000 excess is UBIT. The passive income exclusion for real property rents does not apply to above-FMV payments from controlled entities. Including the entire $200,000 confuses the pre-2006 rule with the current standard.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Churches as automatic public charities
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Grace Community Church has total contributions of $60,000 this year, all from a single wealthy donor family. Despite receiving all support from one source, how is the church classified under Section 509?', '["Private foundation because it fails the one-third public support test", "Private operating foundation because it conducts religious activities", "Public charity that must satisfy the facts and circumstances test", "Public charity because churches are automatically classified as public charities regardless of funding sources"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and Section 170(b)(1)(A)(i), churches are automatically classified as public charities regardless of their funding sources or concentration. Unlike other 501(c)(3) organizations, churches do not need to satisfy the one-third public support test or any other support test to avoid private foundation status. This automatic classification also applies to schools and hospitals. The private foundation presumption does not apply to entities specifically listed in Section 170(b)(1)(A).', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Dissolution clause requirement
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the dissolution clause requirement for obtaining Section 501(c)(3) tax-exempt status?', '["The organization''s governing documents must provide that upon dissolution, remaining assets will be distributed to another exempt organization or to the government", "The organization must dissolve within 10 years of receiving exempt status and redistribute all assets", "The organization must file a dissolution plan with the IRS within 90 days of incorporation", "The organization''s members must vote annually on whether to continue operations or dissolve"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3) and Treas. Reg. 1.501(c)(3)-1(b)(4), the organizational test requires a dissolution clause in the governing documents specifying that assets will be distributed to another 501(c)(3) organization or to a governmental entity upon dissolution. This prevents private individuals from receiving the organization''s assets when it ceases to exist. The requirement to file a dissolution plan within 90 days is fictitious and confuses dissolution provisions with operational filing obligations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Personal property rents and 10% incidental threshold
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Meadowbrook Education Trust, a 501(c)(3), leases an office building to a commercial tenant. The lease provides for $90,000 annual rent for the real property and $15,000 for furniture and office equipment included with the lease. How is the personal property rent treated for UBIT purposes?', '["Both amounts are excluded because all rent from leased property is passive income", "The $15,000 personal property rent is included in UBIT because it exceeds 10% of total rent under the lease", "The full $105,000 is included in UBIT because the property is leased to a commercial tenant", "Only $4,500 of personal property rent is included (the amount exceeding 10% of total rent)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(3), rents from real property are generally excluded from UBIT, but rents from personal property are included unless they are incidental — meaning less than 10% of total rents under the lease. Here, the $15,000 personal property rent equals 14.3% of total rent ($105,000), exceeding the 10% threshold. Therefore, the full $15,000 personal property rent is included in UBIT while the $90,000 real property rent remains excluded. If personal property rent had been under 10%, the entire rental amount would qualify for the passive income exclusion.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Form 990 late filing penalty calculation
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Maple Street Community Services, a 501(c)(3) with annual gross receipts of $350,000, files its Form 990 forty-five days late without reasonable cause. Under IRC Section 6652(c), what penalty does Maple Street face?', '["$450 (45 days at $10 per day)", "$4,500 (45 days at $100 per day)", "No penalty because the organization is tax-exempt", "$900 (45 days at $20 per day)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 6652(c)(1)(A), the penalty for late filing of Form 990 is $20 per day for each day the return is late, up to the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts for organizations with gross receipts under $1,186,000. For Maple Street: 45 × $20 = $900, which is under the $10,500 cap and under 5% of $350,000 ($17,500). The $100/day rate applies only to organizations with gross receipts of $1,186,000 or more, not to organizations of this size.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Additional 200% tax for uncorrected excess benefit
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Clearwater Housing Alliance, a 501(c)(3), pays its CFO an $800,000 bonus that exceeds reasonable compensation by $500,000. The IRS assesses an initial excise tax under Section 4958. If the CFO fails to correct the excess benefit within the taxable period, what additional excise tax is imposed?', '["25% of the excess benefit ($125,000)", "10% of the excess benefit ($50,000)", "200% of the excess benefit ($1,000,000)", "100% of the excess benefit ($500,000)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 4958(b), if a disqualified person fails to correct an excess benefit transaction within the taxable period, an additional excise tax of 200% of the excess benefit is imposed — here, 200% × $500,000 = $1,000,000. The initial tax is 25% of the excess benefit ($125,000), but the 200% additional tax applies when the excess benefit is not timely returned to the organization. The 10% rate is the tax imposed on organization managers who knowingly participate, not on the disqualified person receiving the excess benefit.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Supporting organization and intermediate sanctions analysis
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Alliance for Youth Development operates exclusively to raise funds for three public charities that serve at-risk youth. Alliance is governed by a five-member board where three members are appointed by the supported charities. The organization directs 95% of grants to the supported charities. The remaining $200,000 is paid as consulting fees to a firm owned by one of the supported charity''s executives who also serves on Alliance''s board. The board did not obtain a comparability analysis before approving these payments. Evaluate the tax implications under Section 4958.', '["The payments are permissible because they constitute less than 5% of Alliance''s total expenditures", "The payments are not subject to Section 4958 because Alliance is a supporting organization rather than a standalone 501(c)(3)", "The payments are automatically considered private inurement requiring immediate revocation of Alliance''s tax-exempt status", "The payments trigger excess benefit transaction rules because the executive is a disqualified person and the absence of comparability data eliminates the rebuttable presumption of reasonableness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 4958, excess benefit transaction rules apply to all 501(c)(3) and 501(c)(4) organizations, including supporting organizations. A board member of a supported charity who also serves on Alliance''s board is a disqualified person with substantial influence. Without an independent comparability analysis and documented board deliberation, the organization cannot establish the rebuttable presumption of reasonableness under Treas. Reg. 53.4958-6. Section 4958 provides graduated intermediate sanctions — 25% initial tax on the disqualified person — as an alternative to automatic revocation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Complex multi-activity UBIT analysis with multiple exclusions
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Oakridge Science Museum, a 501(c)(3), reports the following 2025 income: (1) $120,000 from a year-round cafeteria primarily serving museum visitors and staff, (2) $45,000 from renting exhibit hall space during two annual corporate events, (3) $85,000 from selling donated artwork at fundraising auctions, and (4) $30,000 from a parking lot operated daily by paid employees for the general public. Evaluate which income streams are subject to UBIT after applying all relevant exclusions.', '["The $120,000 cafeteria and $30,000 parking lot income are both subject to UBIT", "All four income streams are subject to UBIT because none are related to the museum''s educational purpose", "The $45,000 event rental and $30,000 parking lot income are both subject to UBIT", "Only the $30,000 parking lot income is subject to UBIT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 512, each activity must be analyzed under the three-part test and statutory exclusions. The cafeteria qualifies for the convenience exclusion (Section 513(a)(2)) because it primarily serves museum visitors and staff. The donated artwork sales qualify for the donated goods exclusion (Section 513(a)(3)). The two annual corporate rentals are not "regularly carried on" since they lack the frequency and continuity of comparable commercial venues. Only the parking lot — operated daily by paid employees for the general public — meets all three UBIT conditions with no applicable exclusion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Private inurement prohibition definition
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What does the private inurement prohibition mean for a Section 501(c)(3) organization?', '["No part of the organization''s net earnings may benefit any private shareholder or individual who has a personal interest in the organization", "The organization may not earn any profit from its activities", "The organization must distribute all revenue to charitable beneficiaries each year", "The organization may not pay salaries to its employees or directors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), no part of a tax-exempt organization''s net earnings may inure to the benefit of any private shareholder or individual — meaning insiders such as founders, directors, or officers. This prohibition prevents organizational assets from enriching those with a personal and private interest in the organization. It does not prohibit earning revenue or paying reasonable compensation to employees. Confusing private inurement with a general prohibition on profit or salary payments misunderstands the targeted nature of this restriction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Exempt purposes enumeration under 501(c)(3)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which of the following is one of the exempt purposes recognized under Section 501(c)(3)?', '["Providing commercial banking services to underserved communities", "Testing for public safety", "Operating a for-profit investment fund for charitable donors", "Lobbying for legislation as a primary organizational activity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), testing for public safety is one of the specifically enumerated exempt purposes, along with religious, charitable, scientific, literary, educational purposes, fostering amateur sports competition, and preventing cruelty to children or animals. Commercial banking and operating for-profit investment funds are not exempt purposes. While limited lobbying is permitted, conducting lobbying as a primary activity violates the operational test and is not itself an exempt purpose.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — NOL deduction for UBIT limited to UBI items
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Heritage Arts League, a 501(c)(3), had $50,000 in unrelated business taxable income in 2025 from operating a commercial printing service. It had a $20,000 net operating loss from its printing operations in 2024. Additionally, the League had a $30,000 overall organizational operating deficit in 2024 from its exempt activities. What NOL deduction may Heritage claim against its 2025 UBIT?', '["$50,000, offsetting all of the 2025 UBIT", "$30,000, limited to the deficit from exempt activities", "$20,000, limited to the NOL from unrelated business activities", "$0, because tax-exempt organizations cannot carry forward net operating losses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(6), the NOL deduction for UBIT is computed using only items from unrelated business activities. Losses from exempt activities cannot offset unrelated business income. Heritage''s $20,000 NOL from 2024 printing operations qualifies as a UBI-related NOL and may offset 2025 UBIT, but the $30,000 deficit from exempt activities is excluded from the calculation. The claim that exempt organizations cannot carry forward NOLs is incorrect — the Code specifically allows NOL carryforwards computed under UBI rules.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Form 990-PF required for all private foundations regardless of size
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Cooper Family Foundation, a private foundation with gross receipts of only $35,000 and total assets of $200,000, asks its accountant whether it qualifies to file Form 990-EZ or the electronic Form 990-N instead of a full return. Which form must Cooper file?', '["Form 990-EZ because gross receipts are under $200,000 and assets are under $500,000", "Form 990-N (e-Postcard) because gross receipts are under $50,000", "No annual return is required for private foundations with gross receipts under $50,000", "Form 990-PF, because all private foundations must file this form regardless of size"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 6033(a)(1), every private foundation is required to file Form 990-PF regardless of its gross receipts or total assets. The 990-EZ and 990-N alternatives are available only to public charities, not to private foundations. Even small private foundations with minimal activity must file the full 990-PF, which includes detailed reporting on excise tax computations, grants, and investment activities. The claim that no filing is required confuses private foundation rules with the small public charity thresholds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L3 — Debt-financed income percentage calculation under Section 514
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Sentinel Education Foundation, a 501(c)(3), purchased an office building for $1,000,000 using $700,000 in mortgage debt. The building generates $120,000 in annual rental income from commercial tenants. At year-end, the average acquisition indebtedness is $600,000 and the average adjusted basis of the property is $800,000. Under Section 514, how much of the rental income is subject to UBIT as debt-financed income?', '["$120,000, because any debt-financed rental property is fully taxable", "$90,000 ($120,000 multiplied by $600,000 divided by $800,000)", "$84,000 ($120,000 multiplied by $700,000 divided by $1,000,000)", "$72,000 ($120,000 multiplied by $600,000 divided by $1,000,000)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 514(a)(1), debt-financed income is subject to UBIT in proportion to the average acquisition indebtedness divided by the average adjusted basis of the property. The debt/basis percentage is $600,000 / $800,000 = 75%. Applying this to the $120,000 rental income yields $90,000 subject to UBIT. The $84,000 figure incorrectly uses original debt ($700,000) over original cost ($1,000,000) rather than the required averages. The $72,000 figure incorrectly uses the original purchase price as the denominator instead of average adjusted basis.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Bingo exclusion conditions from UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Valley Community Center, a 501(c)(3), operates weekly bingo games that generate $40,000 in annual net income. Commercial bingo halls also operate in the same jurisdiction. Under IRC Section 513(f), is the bingo income subject to UBIT?', '["No, because bingo income is always excluded from UBIT for tax-exempt organizations", "No, because the bingo games are conducted for an exempt purpose", "Yes, because bingo income is subject to UBIT when commercial bingo is ordinarily carried on in the same jurisdiction", "Yes, because the bingo games generate more than $1,000 in net income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 513(f), bingo games are excluded from UBIT only when commercial bingo is not ordinarily carried on in the same jurisdiction. Because Valley''s jurisdiction has commercial bingo halls operating, the exclusion does not apply and the $40,000 net income is subject to UBIT. The blanket exclusion claim is incorrect — the exclusion is conditional on the absence of commercial bingo in the area. The $1,000 threshold is the specific deduction against total UBIT, not a separate threshold for bingo income specifically.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Convenience exclusion application for campus services
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Prairie View University, a 501(c)(3), operates a campus laundry facility that serves students living in dormitories. The laundry uses coin-operated machines and generates $60,000 in annual revenue. A local laundromat argues this competes unfairly with taxable businesses. Under the UBIT convenience exclusion, how should Prairie View treat the laundry income?', '["The $60,000 is excluded from UBIT under the convenience exclusion because the laundry is operated primarily for the convenience of students", "The $60,000 is subject to UBIT because operating a laundry is a commercial activity unrelated to education", "Only the portion of revenue attributable to non-student users is subject to UBIT", "The $60,000 is excluded only if annual revenue stays below $100,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 513(a)(2), activities conducted primarily for the convenience of members, students, patients, or employees are excluded from UBIT even if they meet the three-part test. Prairie View''s campus laundry primarily serves dormitory students, qualifying for the convenience exclusion. The competitive impact on local businesses is irrelevant to the analysis — the exclusion is based on who the activity primarily serves, not whether it creates commercial competition. There is no $100,000 revenue threshold for the convenience exclusion.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Research exclusion limitations for non-university entities
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Cascade Research Institute, a standalone 501(c)(3) scientific organization that is not a college, university, or hospital, conducts pharmaceutical research under a $2,000,000 contract with a private drug manufacturer. The research results are proprietary to the manufacturer and not available to the public. Under the UBIT research exclusion, is the contract research income subject to UBIT?', '["Yes, because the results are proprietary, the organization is not a college or university, and the research is not government-funded", "No, because all research conducted by 501(c)(3) scientific organizations is exempt from UBIT", "No, because the research is related to the organization''s exempt scientific purpose", "No, because contract research income is classified as passive royalty income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Sections 512(b)(7)-(9), research is excluded from UBIT when performed for the U.S. or a state government, performed by a college, university, or hospital for any person, or the results are freely available to the general public. Cascade is not a college, university, or hospital; the research is not government-funded; and the results are proprietary. None of the three research exclusions apply. The blanket exclusion claim confuses the college/university exemption with the narrower rules for other exempt organizations. Relatedness to exempt purpose does not override the UBIT three-part test.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Filing deadline for Form 990
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the filing deadline for Form 990 for a calendar-year tax-exempt organization?', '["March 15", "April 15", "January 31", "May 15"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 6072(e), Form 990 is due on the 15th day of the 5th month after the end of the organization''s tax year. For calendar-year organizations, this is May 15. An automatic 6-month extension is available by filing Form 8868. The March 15 deadline applies to S corporation and partnership returns, and the April 15 deadline applies to individual income tax returns — neither applies to Form 990 filings.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — One-third public support test for public charity status
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Horizon Community Fund, a 501(c)(3), receives $600,000 in total support: $250,000 from government grants, $150,000 from general public contributions where no single donor exceeds 2% of total support, and $200,000 from investment income. Under the one-third support test of Section 509(a)(1), does Horizon qualify as a public charity?', '["Yes, because public support of $400,000 exceeds one-third of total support", "No, because investment income disqualifies the organization from public charity status", "Yes, but only if it also meets the facts and circumstances test", "No, because government grants do not count as public support"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and Section 170(b)(1)(A)(vi), an organization qualifies as a public charity if it normally receives more than one-third of its support from government sources and the general public. Horizon''s public support includes $250,000 in government grants and $150,000 in public contributions, totaling $400,000 — which is 66.7% of total support ($600,000), well exceeding the one-third threshold. Investment income is included in total support but not counted as public support. Government grants explicitly count as public support under this test.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Form 990-T filing threshold based on gross UBI
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Pinecrest Animal Rescue, a 501(c)(3), operates a grooming service that generates $8,500 in gross unrelated business income and incurs $7,200 in directly connected expenses. After the $1,000 specific deduction, Pinecrest''s unrelated business taxable income is $300. Must Pinecrest file Form 990-T, and what is the basis for the filing requirement?', '["No, because UBTI of $300 is below the $1,000 specific deduction threshold", "No, because the grooming service is related to the animal rescue mission", "Yes, because gross unrelated business income of $8,500 meets the $1,000 filing threshold", "Yes, but only if UBTI exceeds $1,000 after all deductions and exclusions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 6012(a)(6), Form 990-T must be filed when an exempt organization has gross unrelated business income of $1,000 or more — the threshold is based on gross income, not net taxable income. Pinecrest''s gross UBI of $8,500 exceeds the $1,000 threshold, requiring a Form 990-T filing even though the net UBTI after expenses and the specific deduction is only $300. The specific deduction reduces taxable income but does not affect the filing obligation. Confusing the gross income filing threshold with net UBTI is a common error.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Foundation manager tax rate for self-dealing participation
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'The board treasurer of Redwood Heritage Foundation, a private foundation, knowingly approves a loan of $100,000 from the foundation to its founder. The loan constitutes self-dealing under Section 4941. What excise tax is imposed on the board treasurer for participating in this transaction?', '["25% of the amount involved ($25,000)", "10% of the amount involved ($10,000)", "200% of the amount involved ($200,000)", "5% of the amount involved ($5,000)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 4941(a)(2), a foundation manager who knowingly participates in an act of self-dealing is subject to an excise tax of 5% of the amount involved, up to a maximum of $20,000 per act. For the $100,000 loan, the tax on the treasurer is $5,000. The 10% rate applies to the self-dealer (the founder who received the loan), not the foundation manager. The 25% rate applies to excess benefit transactions under Section 4958, a different provision from the private foundation self-dealing rules of Section 4941.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Section 4945 taxable expenditures and expenditure responsibility
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Lakewood Community Foundation, a private foundation, awards a $150,000 grant to a 501(c)(4) social welfare organization to fund community health education programs. The foundation does not exercise expenditure responsibility over the grant — it did not require reports, did not segregate grant funds, and did not conduct post-grant monitoring. The board believed that because the grantee was tax-exempt, expenditure responsibility was unnecessary. Evaluate the tax consequences under Section 4945.', '["The grant is not a taxable expenditure because the grantee is a tax-exempt organization under Section 501(c)", "The grant is permissible without expenditure responsibility if the grantee agrees to use funds for charitable purposes", "The grant is a taxable expenditure because the foundation failed to exercise expenditure responsibility over a grant to a non-501(c)(3) organization, triggering a 20% initial excise tax", "The grant is automatically permissible if the total amount is less than $250,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 4945(d)(4) and (h), grants by a private foundation to organizations that are not 501(c)(3) public charities are taxable expenditures unless the foundation exercises expenditure responsibility. This requires segregation of grant funds, periodic reporting by the grantee, and foundation monitoring. A 501(c)(4) organization is not a 501(c)(3), so the public charity exemption does not apply. The initial excise tax is 20% on the foundation ($30,000) plus 5% on any manager who knowingly approved the grant. Tax-exempt status alone does not eliminate the expenditure responsibility requirement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Form 990-N e-Postcard eligibility
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which tax-exempt organizations are eligible to file Form 990-N (e-Postcard)?', '["All tax-exempt organizations regardless of size", "Organizations with annual gross receipts normally $50,000 or less", "Only private foundations with assets under $100,000", "Organizations with total assets under $500,000 regardless of gross receipts"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 6033(i), organizations with annual gross receipts normally $50,000 or less may file Form 990-N (e-Postcard), an electronic notice requiring only basic information such as the organization''s name, address, EIN, and confirmation of continued eligibility. Private foundations must file Form 990-PF regardless of size and are not eligible for the 990-N. The $500,000 asset threshold is part of the Form 990-EZ eligibility test, not the e-Postcard threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Excess business holdings combined ownership analysis
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Thornton Family Foundation, a private foundation, owns 8% of the voting stock of DataCore Inc. The foundation''s disqualified persons collectively own 10% of DataCore''s voting stock. An unrelated third party offers to sell an additional 5% stake to the foundation. The foundation''s investment advisor recommends the purchase for portfolio diversification. Evaluate whether the purchase would create an excess business holdings violation under Section 4943.', '["The purchase is permissible because the foundation''s direct holdings of 13% would remain under the 20% threshold", "The purchase is permissible because only the foundation''s own holdings count toward the 20% permitted holding", "The purchase would create excess holdings because combined ownership of 23% would exceed the 20% permitted holding, triggering a 10% initial excise tax on the excess", "The purchase would be prohibited only if the foundation acquires majority control of DataCore"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 4943(c)(2), a private foundation and its disqualified persons together may not hold more than 20% of the voting stock of a corporation (or 35% if unrelated parties have effective control). After the purchase, combined holdings would be 13% (foundation) + 10% (disqualified persons) = 23%, exceeding the 20% permitted holding by 3%. The initial excise tax under Section 4943(a)(1) is 10% of the value of the excess business holdings. The 20% threshold includes all disqualified persons'' holdings — not just the foundation''s direct ownership.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Section 4940 excise tax rate on net investment income
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What excise tax rate applies to a private foundation''s net investment income under Section 4940?', '["2%", "5%", "10%", "1.39%"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 4940(a), private foundations are subject to a 1.39% excise tax on their net investment income, which includes interest, dividends, rents, royalties, and net capital gains. This rate was established by the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2019, replacing the prior two-tier 2%/1% structure. The 2% rate was the former default rate before the 2019 simplification. The 10% rate applies to self-dealing transactions under Section 4941, not to net investment income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Private benefit doctrine scope
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the private benefit doctrine as applied to Section 501(c)(3) organizations?', '["An organization must not provide more than incidental benefits to any private individual, whether or not that person is an insider", "An organization may provide unlimited benefits to employees as part of their compensation package", "Benefits provided to the general public automatically satisfy the private benefit standard", "The private benefit doctrine applies only to transactions involving the organization''s founders"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3) and the operational test, the private benefit doctrine prohibits an exempt organization from providing more than incidental benefit to any private individual, regardless of whether that person is an insider. This is broader than the private inurement prohibition, which applies only to insiders such as founders, directors, and officers. The doctrine is violated when private benefit exceeds what is incidental to the public benefit, even when the benefited individual has no relationship to the organization.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Form 8868 extension for Form 990
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What form does a tax-exempt organization use to request an automatic extension of time to file Form 990?', '["Form 4868", "Form 7004", "Form 8868", "Form 2553"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 6081 and Treas. Reg. 1.6081-9, Form 8868 provides an automatic 6-month extension for tax-exempt organizations to file Form 990, 990-EZ, 990-PF, and 990-T. Form 4868 is the extension form for individual income tax returns (Form 1040), and Form 7004 is used by corporations and partnerships for their income tax returns. Form 2553 is the S corporation election form and has no connection to filing extensions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Intermediate sanctions correction period and total excise tax analysis
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Dr. Williams, the medical director and board member of Sunrise Health Clinic (a 501(c)(3)), received $1,200,000 in total compensation in 2024. The IRS determines in 2025 that reasonable compensation is $700,000, creating a $500,000 excess benefit. The board approved the package without obtaining comparability data or documenting its deliberations. Dr. Williams repays the $500,000 excess plus interest in January 2026, before the IRS issues a statutory notice of deficiency. Analyze the total excise tax exposure for Dr. Williams and the organization managers.', '["Dr. Williams owes the 25% initial tax ($125,000) because timely correction eliminates only the 200% additional tax; participating managers owe 10% up to $20,000 each", "Dr. Williams owes both the 25% initial tax and the 200% additional tax ($1,125,000 total) because the rebuttable presumption was not established", "Dr. Williams owes no excise tax because full repayment of the excess benefit corrects the transaction and eliminates all penalties", "Dr. Williams owes the 25% initial tax but managers face no liability because they relied on their own judgment when setting compensation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 4958, the 25% initial tax ($125,000) applies to the $500,000 excess benefit regardless of subsequent correction. Because Dr. Williams corrected the excess benefit by repaying before the IRS issued a statutory notice of deficiency, the 200% additional tax is avoided per Section 4958(b). Organization managers who knowingly participated face a 10% tax up to $20,000 per transaction under Section 4958(a)(2). The absence of comparability data prevents the rebuttable presumption of reasonableness but does not increase the tax rate. Correction eliminates only the 200% additional tax, not the 25% initial tax.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Section 4942 distributable amount calculation with 4940 offset
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Heritage Arts Foundation, a private foundation, has non-charitable-use assets valued at $6,000,000 at year-end. During the year, the foundation distributed $200,000 for charitable purposes and paid $10,000 in Section 4940 excise tax on its net investment income. Under Section 4942, what is the foundation''s undistributed income and the initial excise tax on the underdistribution?', '["Undistributed income is $100,000 and the initial excise tax is $30,000", "Undistributed income is $90,000 and the initial excise tax is $27,000", "Undistributed income is $100,000 and the initial excise tax is $10,000", "No underdistribution exists because the Section 4940 tax is credited dollar-for-dollar against the distribution requirement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 4942, the distributable amount equals 5% of non-charitable-use assets ($300,000) minus the Section 4940 excise tax paid ($10,000), yielding a distributable amount of $290,000. Since the foundation distributed only $200,000, the underdistribution is $90,000. The initial excise tax is 30% of the undistributed amount: 30% × $90,000 = $27,000. The $100,000 figure ignores the Section 4940 tax reduction that decreases the distributable amount. The 30% initial excise tax rate applies to underdistributions, not the 10% rate used for excess business holdings.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
